TWO PEER-REVIEWED DRIVERS, CONVERGING ON THE SAME GAP.

(1) Cantor J, Huang E, Zhang LA, Salas A, Zhang F, Burnett A, Lohn A, Pataranutaporn P, Rader B,
    Yu H, McBain RK. "Suicide-Related Responses From AI Chatbots Through Application Programming
    Interfaces and Consumer-Facing Apps: Observational Cross-Sectional Comparative Study."
    J Med Internet Res 2026;28:e97968. doi 10.2196/97968, PMID 42855144.
    RAND Corporation / Harvard Pilgrim / Harvard Medical School / MIT Media Lab / Mass General
    Brigham. PubMed-verified 15 Oct 2026.

    Design: five consumer models (ChatGPT, Claude, Gemini, Grok, Llama) x 30 previously vetted
    suicide-related prompts x five clinician-assigned risk levels, each submitted 100 times through
    BOTH the public-facing UI and direct API access = 30,000 responses. Blinded LLM classifier
    validated against independent human coding (289/300, 96.3%, Cohen kappa 0.89). Mixed-effects
    logistic regression.

    Findings:
      - Direct responses: 77.4% via API vs 62.2% via UI.
      - **UI access carried lower odds of a direct response: OR 0.09, 95% CI 0.08-0.10.**
      - Divergence widest at the top of the risk gradient: very-high-risk prompts 24.8% direct via
        API vs 4.6% via UI; high-risk 80.1% vs 48.4%.
      - Gemini ran the other way (UI 76.7% vs API 71.0%), so the direction is not universal.
      - Authors' conclusion, verbatim: "AI safety depends on how users access the model. Safety
        observed in a UI should not be assumed to generalize to API access or apps built on APIs.
        Evaluations and policies should consider access modes."

(2) Chen D, Blayne A, Ning T, Chattha I, Afshar M, Ong JCL, Hong JC, Liu N, Celi LA, Moons KGM,
    Collins GS, Bitterman DS. "Reporting completeness of clinical trials evaluating large language
    model interventions in medicine: systematic review." BMJ 2026;395:e100855.
    doi 10.1136/bmj-2026-100855, PMID 42869550. PubMed-verified 15 Oct 2026. Author list includes
    the TRIPOD and CONSORT methodologists.

    461 records (68 trial reports, 15 protocols, 378 registrations) scored against TRIPOD-LLM,
    CONSORT 2025 / CONSORT-AI, SPIRIT 2025 / SPIRIT-AI, CHART, and the WHO registration dataset.
      - Mean completeness: **TRIPOD-LLM 40%**, CONSORT 2025 44%, CONSORT-AI 59%, CHART 52%;
        protocols SPIRIT 2025 51% / SPIRIT-AI 57%; registrations 61%.
      - Inconsistently reported critical elements, verbatim, include "data and intervention
        provenance, input preprocessing, **model versioning and access modality**, inference
        parameters, output postprocessing, as well as protocol, data, and code access."
      - "**Harms planning, monitoring, and reporting were often incompletely reported**, limiting
        the critical appraisal of the safety of LLM interventions in healthcare."

    NOTE: this review is NOT YET IN THE REFERENCE LIBRARY — the daily scan missed it (see the review
    record). It is cited here on direct PubMed verification.

Why they converge: Cantor measures how much safety behaviour depends on access modality; Chen names
access modality as one of the elements LLM trials routinely fail to report. One supplies the effect
size, the other supplies the reporting obligation.

