NOT PROPOSED — recorded so the reasoning is auditable

1. Tiryaki EB, Tiryaki O, Soysal M, Kanbay Y, Tufekci FG. "Agreement and classification performance
   of ChatGPT-5-generated and nurse-led emergency department triage using an expert reference
   decision." Int Emerg Nurs 2026;89:101918. doi 10.1016/j.ienj.2026.101918, PMID 42863428.
   PubMed-verified. **The most quotable "LLM beats humans at triage" result to date, and the PI
   should expect to be asked about it.** 1,564 adult ED patients: ChatGPT-5 agreed with the expert
   reference better than nurses (kappa 0.670 vs 0.467), with higher accuracy (83.5% vs 73.3%),
   higher sensitivity (82.0% vs 49.0%), and FEWER under-triage errors (18.0% vs 51.0% of
   expert-yellow patients mislabelled green).
   Why it does NOT bear on this protocol's premise, and why no edit follows:
     (a) **It contains no high-acuity cases.** The cohort is "1564 adult patients with documented
         yellow or green triage classifications." The primary endpoint here is the Critical Miss Rate
         on presentations requiring emergency escalation. A cohort with no red cases is structurally
         unable to speak to it.
     (b) **The input is structured clinical data**, not lay language: "Standardized triage-level
         patient information was entered into ChatGPT-5 using an identical prompt." That is the
         informational regime the §0 DECISION 1 note already identifies as decisive, on the
         strength of Jia et al. (objective data eliminated emergency under-triage, 25% -> 0%).
     (c) The human comparator is triage nurses; Arm B is emergency physicians.
   Retrospective, single-centre. It belongs in the anticipated-questions brief, not in the protocol.

2. Glasner S, Michero D, Stewart M. "Human-in-the-Loop Suicide Risk Detection and Clinical
   Escalation in a Generative AI Mental Health Chatbot: Prospective Implementation Study." JMIR Form
   Res 2026. doi 10.2196/106118, PMID 42853564. PubMed-verified. **ALSO MISSING FROM THE LIBRARY — a
   second reconciler miss this week.** Prospective, two phases: clinicians successfully reached only
   25/81 (30.9%) then 73/172 (42.4%) of participants whose suicide-risk alert fired, at a mean 11-12
   minutes to outreach initiation.
   Why it is worth the PI's attention but not an edit: it quantifies something §10's S2/S3 boundary
   gestures at — **"escalate to a human" is an endpoint whose downstream completion rate is well
   under half**, even in a purpose-built product with a dedicated clinical team. Our instrument
   scores whether the model recommends escalation, not whether escalation happens. That is already
   inside the stated S2/S3 boundary, so it needs no new text; it is good supporting material if the
   PI ever writes the successor-study section.

